3. Vulnerability Scanning (OpenVAS / Nessus)

3.1 Concepts
A vulnerability scanner tests hosts against a large database of known weaknesses and reports them with severity.
Nmap vs vulnerability scanner: Nmap shows what is running. The scanner shows what is vulnerable.
Vulnerability scan vs penetration test: the scan detects, a pentest exploits and proves impact.
CVE: unique ID for a public vulnerability (e.g. CVE-2011-2523).
CVSS: score from 0 to 10 for severity.
NVT (Network Vulnerability Test): one check in OpenVAS’s feed.
Authenticated vs unauthenticated scan: authenticated scans log in and find far more (missing patches), unauthenticated scans see only what’s exposed to the network.
False positives: the scanner may report something that isn’t real, so verify important findings manually.

3.2 OpenVAS (Greenbone) Components
Component	             Role
gvmd	                 Manager, handles tasks, targets, and reports
ospd-openvas / openvas	 The scanner engine
gsad	                 Web interface (port 9392)
PostgreSQL	             Database
Redis	                 Fast storage during scans
Feeds (NVT, SCAP, CERT)	Vulnerability data that must stay updated

3.3 Workflow
Install and set up: sudo apt install gvm, sudo gvm-setup, sudo gvm-check-setup, sudo gvm-start.
Log in at https://127.0.0.1:9392.
Wait for the feeds to finish loading.
Create a Target (IP of Metasploitable2) and a Task (e.g. “Full and fast” scan config), or use the Task Wizard.
Run the task and wait until the status is Done.
Open the Report and download as PDF or HTML.

3.4 Severity Levels (CVSS v3)
Level	Score	Meaning
Critical	9.0 to 10.0	Easily exploitable, severe impact. Fix immediately
High	7.0 to 8.9	Serious. Fix soon
Medium	4.0 to 6.9	Moderate risk, often needs conditions
Low	0.1 to 3.9	Minor
Log / Info	0	Informational



